+
+## Its own mastermind (ask8-session-mastermind, 2026-11-03)
+Each voice session gets its own standing `mastermind` (role `mastermind`, path `public/framework/ux/Dictate/`), not the one shared `mastermind-voice-system`, so it can `follow` just that session's own log instead of being told every sentence. It is spawned the first time the single seat wakes (and again on a resume that never got one — `Sessions.js`: `ensure_mastermind()`, wired into `wake()`), held open with no prompt like the seats, and its id lives on the session record (`s.mastermind`) so later wakes reuse it. The seat's brief substitutes `{{mastermind}}` for this id (`single_brief(s)`); a failed spawn falls back to the old shared `mastermind-voice-system` so the turn still works. Its own brief: `Servex/agents/session-mastermind.md` (`mastermind_brief(s)`), under 250 words, substituted with the session's id, log file and page dir. Teardown: the idle sweep's `sleep()` stops it too, though it normally holds no process open on its own (`dormant_after: 0`). Proof: `Servex/agents/Sessions.mastermind.test.mjs`.
 
 ## Where each piece is
 - Role row `single-voice-assistant`: `Servex/agents/roles.js`; model tier `voice_single` (Sonnet, effort low): `Servex/agents/tiers.js`.
 - The proof: `Servex/agents/Sessions.single.test.mjs` (plain node, no model). It runs every `js` example in the brief for real against a temp session file, then replays the file the way Chat V3 reads it (cleaned words, title, paragraphs, raw kept). `Servex/agents/single-brief.test.mjs` proves the brief itself: its own words, the real opening prompt (`brief() + where`) a seat is actually spawned with, and a replayed real session driven through the real `say`/`nothing`/`retract_say` handlers in `tools.js`.
 
